    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI extended the business-day rule for registration dates so it now rolls forward past public holidays as well as weekends.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New `PublicHolidays.java`** (`model/`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility class that owns the fixed holiday calendar (2026-01-01, 2026-01-26, 2026-04-25, 2026-12-25, 2026-12-28) as an immutable `Set<LocalDate>`, exposing one `contains(date)` check. The calendar is separated from the rolling logic so each has one reason to change.\n\n2. **`BusinessDay.java`** \u2014 `rollForward` now advances day-by-day until it lands on a date that is neither a weekend nor a public holiday, delegating the holiday check to `PublicHolidays`. This iterative approach naturally handles chains (e.g. a holiday adjacent to a weekend, or the consecutive 12-25/12-28 pair) that the old two-day weekend jump couldn't.\n\nThe single call site (`Owner.normalizeRegistrationDate`) is unchanged \u2014 it already routes through `BusinessDay.rollForward`, so all callers get the new behavior for free. The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
